Show the student an interactive step-by-step explanation: a diagram that stays in place and a panel that walks through one change per step. Use it when a mechanism, process, decomposition or relationship is easier to follow as a sequence than as text, and at most once per reply.

A separate writer builds the explanation from your brief. It sees neither this conversation nor the course material, so the brief carries everything it needs: the concept and what the student should understand or where they went wrong, the key relationships and formulas with the course's notation and terms, illustrative numbers (from the course material when it has them), and the course documents it draws on with title and page or section.

Still answer in text: one or two sentences that say what the explanation shows, with your usual [n] citations. If the explanation cannot be built, explain in text instead.
